# Patch 0009 — skill: a shell write whose target is not a literal path is refused in a project that tracks files
# Offered back: no (romp-only)
# Files: skill/SKILL.md
#
# Reason: romp's own PreToolUse hook on the Bash tool (hooks/romp-track-bash-guard.mjs; plans/file-review.md,
# decision 47) refuses a shell command that would write a tracked file. Until 2026-09-18 it judged only a
# target it could read: a `cp "$SRC" "$DST"` passed and overwrote a tracked file with no change recorded, while
# the same cp spelled out was refused (a research session's report through the box admin, 2026-09-17). The hook
# now refuses such a target while a project that tracks anything is in play, and reads none of the agent's
# variables to resolve it (HOME alone, for `~` and a leading `$HOME`, as the shell does). The "When tracking is
# ON" section gains one paragraph that tells the agent so and what to do (spell the path out; a tracked file's
# change goes through track-edit), so the refusal it meets is no surprise, and names the one exception the
# round-1 review of that change (2026-09-18) allowed, as its round 2 the same day corrected it: a target whose
# only expansions are `$$`, `$RANDOM` or `$SECONDS` (the last two not inside a script handed to `sh`, where dash
# lets a script assign them; `$BASHPID`, which round 1 listed, never, since zsh lets a command assign it) at an
# absolute path outside the project, which cannot reach back into it. Other hosts have no Bash-side guard, so
# this stays romp's. Prose only; pinned by the host's tools/vendor-patches.test.mjs (P9) and the drift test.
diff --git a/skill/SKILL.md b/skill/SKILL.md
index f7d8bc9..d1df78e 100644
--- a/skill/SKILL.md
+++ b/skill/SKILL.md
@@ -59,6 +59,17 @@ heredoc included), no `sed -i` or `perl -i`, no python `open(..., 'w')` or node
 the write: its status is 0 when tracking is ON, so `track-config ... && cp ...` runs
 the copy on exactly the file it must not touch.
 
+In a project that tracks files, a shell write whose target is not a literal path
+is refused as well: a variable such as `"$DST"`, a `$(...)`, a glob or brace list
+the guard cannot expand. The guard cannot tell which file such a word names and
+does not read your variables to find out (it reads `HOME` alone, so a leading
+`$HOME/` is read as `~/` is), so the refusal holds whatever the word would expand to.
+One exception: a target whose only expansions are `$$`, `$RANDOM` or `$SECONDS`
+(the last two not inside a script handed to `sh`, where they are ordinary variables;
+`$BASHPID` never), at an absolute path outside the project, is allowed. Spell the
+path out; a tracked file then takes its change through `track-edit`, and a file
+outside the project takes an ordinary write.
+
 For ANY change to the file, use the CLI, NOT the Edit/Write/MultiEdit tools:
 
 - **Make or replace text** — applies the change AND records it as your tracked
